## Founding & History
Craigow Vineyard was established in 1989 by Dr. Barry Edwards and his family, who planted vines on the sunny slopes overlooking the Coal River. The first vintage was harvested in 1993. Craigow is recognized as one of the first vineyards established in southern Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The property itself has a longer history, originally being a farming estate developed by Scottish doctor James Murdoch in the early 1820s. Murdoch named the property Craigow-by-Milnathort, after his home in Kinross-Shire. He cultivated fruit trees, barley, grazed livestock, and even experimented with growing opium poppies for medical use. Later generations of the Murdoch family developed Craigow into a significant country estate, which once housed Tasmania's largest apricot orchards.

## People
Craigow Vineyard is a family-owned and operated vineyard. The current owners are Dr. Barry Edwards and his family. Barry and Cathy Edwards are noted for transitioning from grapegrowers to producing a portfolio of wines. The winemaker is Tasmanian Vintners, and the viticulturalist is Fred Peacock. Visitors can enjoy an intimate wine tasting with owner Barry Edwards and his family.

## Vineyard & Site
Craigow Vineyard is located in the Coal River Valley, Tasmania. The vineyard area is 10 hectares. The growing conditions in the Coal River Valley are considered ideal for classic cool-climate varieties, similar to cool-climate wine regions of France, Burgundy, Alsace, and the Loire Valley. Tasmania's location in the path of the "Roaring Forties" brings clean air and pure rainwater to the island's vineyards. The vines were planted on sunny slopes overlooking the Coal River. The wines are crafted to reflect the characteristic terroir of the Coal River Valley.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Craigow cellar door is housed in an original converted farm worker's cottage, offering a quiet, scenic, relaxed, and intimate setting for tastings. It overlooks the vineyard's rolling slopes. The cellar door is open Saturday and Sunday from 11 am to 4 pm. On Monday to Friday, the cellar door is open by appointment. Bookings are recommended, especially on weekends, and can be made via email or phone for same-day visits. Buses and coaches are accepted by appointment only. Guests can enjoy a guided tasting of all 8 current releases with owner Barry Edwards and family. The experience is described as informative, with a welcoming and knowledgeable team. The cellar door has a pretty vine-covered patio looking over a small display vineyard.

## Food & Hospitality
The vineyard offers snacks. Some sources indicate a delectable selection of locally sourced gourmet dishes, expertly paired with their handcrafted wines, creating a unique dining experience. However, other sources only mention snacks.

## Awards & Recognition
In 2008, Craigow won the Tasmanian vineyard of the year award. James Halliday notes Craigow for its long-lived Riesling of particular quality, closely followed by Pinot Noir. The 2005 Riesling received a Gold medal in 2007. A sparkling red was a medal winner at the 2009 Tasmanian Wine Show. The Real Review lists several Craigow wines with scores, including a 94 silver for Craigow Pinot Noir 2023 and 93 silver for Craigow White Blend 2023. The cellar door has received a "Dom Hidden Gem" award.

## Sustainability & Certifications
No reliable information found from public sources.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Craigow Vineyard is 528 Richmond Road, Cambridge, TAS, 7170, Australia. It is located in Cambridge, a suburb of Hobart. The vineyard is approximately a 30-minute drive from Hobart. The phone number is 0418 126 027. The website is www.craigow.com.au. Craigow also has a presence on Facebook and Instagram. The cellar door is open Saturday and Sunday 11 am - 4 pm, and other times by appointment. Bookings are recommended.

## Notable / Distinctive
Craigow Vineyard is distinctive for being one of the first vineyards established in southern Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The property has a rich history dating back to the early 1820s, when it was a farming estate developed by Dr. James Murdoch, who even experimented with growing opium poppies. It was also once home to Tasmania's largest apricot orchards. The cellar door is housed in an original farm worker's cottage, providing a historic and intimate tasting environment. The vineyard is family-owned and operated, with owner Barry Edwards often conducting tastings.

